#front[Appendix C. Reading the formal statements]

The mathematical chapters can be read on paper. This appendix explains how to locate the statement of an assertion in the formal development, how to determine its quantifiers, and how to tell which earlier mathematical results supply its hypotheses. The running examples are the textbook statement about zeta, the positive excursion of a prime sum, and the cohomological bound on an interval. All links into the development refer to one source snapshot.

#heading(level:2,numbering:none)[From the Dirichlet series to the library definition]

Start with a formulation independent of any name in a library. Let $D=ℂ backslash {1}$. For every function $f$ holomorphic on $D$ and satisfying
$ f(s)=sum_(n=1)^infinity frac(1,n^s) quad (Re s>1), $
ask that each zero $s$ of $f$ in $D$ satisfy one of two conditions. Either $s=-2m$ for some positive integer $m$, or $Re s=1/2$. This uses only a Dirichlet series, its domain of convergence, and ordinary holomorphicity. It specifies the exact range of the integer indexing the trivial zeros.

Why does quantifying over every $f$ give the usual conjecture? The zeta function obtained by meromorphic continuation is holomorphic on $D$ and satisfies the displayed identity in its half-plane of convergence. If $f$ is any second candidate, the difference between $f$ and zeta is holomorphic on the connected open set $D$ and zero on the entire open half-plane $Re s>1$. The identity theorem makes the difference zero throughout $D$. Connectedness here is a concrete geometric assertion: any two points of the plane other than one can be joined by a polygonal path avoiding one. Holomorphicity and the Dirichlet series therefore specify the same function at every point relevant to the zeros. The value a total function assigns at one can play no part in this argument.

The library formulation asks that if the value of its zeta function at $s$ is zero, if no natural number $n$ satisfies $s=-2(n+1)$, and if $s!=1$, then $Re s=1/2$. The set of values $-2(n+1)$ with $n>=0$ is exactly the set $-2m$ with $m>=1$. In particular zero is not among the trivial zeros. Away from one the library's function is differentiable, and in $Re s>1$ its series may be indexed by $n>=0$ with denominator $(n+1)^s$. Reindexing gives the displayed series. One implication follows by applying the textbook assertion to this particular function. For the other, take an arbitrary textbook candidate and use the identity theorem before applying the library assertion. This proves equivalence without deciding what convention to use for evaluating a meromorphic function at its pole. #link("https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/0df444a360eaa60ab8c11dca51a86af692955474/Mathlib/NumberTheory/LSeries/RiemannZeta.lean")[The library definition and its analytic properties.]

There is also the more common open-strip wording. To pass from the library assertion to the strip wording, a zero with $0<Re s<1$ satisfies both exceptions automatically. For the reverse passage one uses known facts outside the open strip. The Euler product excludes zeros in the right half-plane beyond one, and classical nonvanishing excludes the boundary there. The functional equation handles the left half-plane and its boundary. The value at zero is $-1/2$. These deductions, written out in Chapter 18, show which facts accompany the passage between the two familiar wordings of the conjecture. The library statement incorporates those facts in its range, which is why checking the exceptions is informative.

#heading(level:2,numbering:none)[Five conclusions and their domains]

The public endpoint has five declarations. The first supplies the library's ordinary Riemann hypothesis. The second supplies the directly spelled-out zeta proposition just discussed. In the snapshot both are obtained from the same term. The third supplies nonvanishing of every Dirichlet $L$-function to the right of one half. The fourth places all its zeros in the *open critical strip* on the middle line. The fifth specializes the strip statement to primitive characters. The domains can be compared at two points. The half-plane assertion tests $1+i t$ for $t!=0$, which the strip assertion omits. The strip assertion rules out a zero at $1/3+i t$, which the half-plane assertion by itself does not address. The functional equation and the nonvanishing results at the boundary connect the two formulations. Both leave the imaginary axis outside their hypotheses. #src("Statements")[The four named propositions.] #src("Main")[Their five public declarations.]

Consider the half-plane proposition more closely. Its quantifiers range over a natural number $N$ with a nonzero witness, a character modulo $N$, and a complex number $s$. The hypotheses on this point are
$ Re s>1/2, quad (chi!=chi_0 " or " s!=1). $
Here $chi_0$ is the principal character at that modulus. The disjunction excludes exactly the joint event $chi=chi_0$ and $s=1$. In particular, a nonprincipal character may be evaluated at one. Treating the condition as if it excluded every such point would discard the classical nonvanishing statement used at the end of Chapter 7. The nonzero-modulus witness admits $N=1$, where the unique character gives ordinary zeta.

For the imprimitive step, read the finite product from the point of view of inequalities. If $p$ divides a larger modulus and $Re s>1/2$, then $abs(p^(-s))=p^(-Re s)<1$. A character value has modulus at most one. It follows that $chi^(*)(p)p^(-s)$ cannot equal one. The finite product of the correction factors is therefore nonzero. Multiplying by the nonzero value for the inducing character gives the nonzero value at the larger modulus. This reasoning checks both positivity of the integer prime and strictness of the real-part inequality; it would give no strict bound on the imaginary axis. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.CharacterBoxes.Imprimitive")[Passage from primitive to arbitrary characters.] #src("Formalism.GammaFactorNonvanishing.CritLine")[The open-strip reflection.]

A simple way to read any of these statements is to test its inclusions. Try $s=0$, $s=1$, $s=-2$ and a proposed zero with real part $0.6$. Check separately a primitive nonprincipal character, its change to a larger modulus, and the character of modulus one. The zeta statement excludes the pole and the negative even integers from its premise. The open-strip statement omits all three real test points immediately. A proposed zero at $0.6+i t$ is ruled out by the half-plane statement at every modulus. These tests detect differences in quantification that the short names of the declarations do not display.

#heading(level:2,numbering:none)[The analytic implication as a reusable theorem]

The localized theorem has a family $F$, three real parameters $theta,delta,C$, hypotheses $0<theta<1$ and $delta>0$, and a single statement about arbitrarily distant windows. Written with the upper-bound function denoted by $G_F$, the last hypothesis is
$ forall Y_0, exists Y>=Y_0, forall y in [Y,Y^(1+delta)], quad G_(F)(y)<=C y^theta. $
The consequent, `ZeroFree F θ`, quantifies over every family index $i$ and every complex $s$ with $theta<Re s$, including $Re s>=1$. Admissibility requires each modulus to be positive and each member to have modulus one or be nonprincipal. Inside the strip the proof uses the localized pole argument of Chapter 9. For $Re s>=1$ it invokes the library nonvanishing theorem. In the modulus-one branch this includes the library's nonzero total-function value at $s=1$. The public theorem for arbitrary characters explicitly omits the principal pole and requires no interpretation of a meromorphic value there. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.DedekindLFunctions.CharFamily")[The family and its zero-free property.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.DedekindLFunctions.LandauTheorems.lemmaL")[The split into the two domains.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.DedekindLFunctions.LandauTheorems.CharFamilyLFunctionNeZeroOfOneLeRe")[The boundary theorem.]

The same $F$ determines the prime statistic in the hypothesis and the functions in the conclusion. If a different family could be chosen after each interval, its small statistic would give no information about a pole of the original family. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.DedekindLFunctions.LandauTheorems.ZeroFreeOfRpowWindow")[The one-sided implication.]

To trace the proof of that implication in reverse, first look at the passage from the pointwise upper bound to a weighted integral estimate. If $G_F(y)<=C y^theta$ on a window and $u=log y$, multiplying by $e^(-theta u)$ leaves a constant majorant for its positive part. On the log interval this costs at most $max(C,0)delta log Y$. For each fixed $eta>0$, that quantity is eventually below $exp(eta log Y)$. This is how a pointwise estimate gives the slightly more flexible weighted hypothesis in the source. A lower bound on the statistic would not imply this majorization. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.DedekindLFunctions.LandauTheorems.WindowHypOfRpow")[Conversion of the hypotheses.]

The other ingredient compares two analytically continued germs, one at a real center and one at a suitable shifted center. Only in the real-pole case does the sign of a residue replace the modulus comparison between centers. If the coefficient of one family member were negative, its contribution could alter that sign or cancel the pole at a shared zero. A change as small as replacing one average of real parts by an alternating sum would therefore require a new pole calculation. Looking backwards through the analytic theorem reveals why the arithmetic construction has to prescribe its Fourier coefficients in advance. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.DedekindLFunctions.LandauTheorems.poleData")[Poles and their residues.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.DedekindLFunctions.LandauTheorems.LandauDichotomy")[The alternatives for the nearest pole.]

#heading(level:2,numbering:none)[Reading a global length bound]

Fix a field $E$, an integer cutoff $t$, and a real number $B$. The assertion called #emph[VVle] first quantifies over algebraic closures and compatible local cohomological data. For every such choice it requires the following two properties. Each individual length at a prime $ell$ and positive twist $j<t$ is finite. For every *finite* set $S$ of primes one then has
$ sum_(ell in S)(log ell)sum_(j=1)^(t-1) d_(E)(ell,j)<=B. $
Here $d_(E)(ell,j)$ denotes the length, measured as a nonnegative integer. In this display $ell$ in the arguments and logarithm denotes the rational prime. The expression is the same weighted subtotal used in Chapter 16. In the source, finiteness is stated before unwrapping an extended-natural length into an ordinary integer. A bound on a numerical image alone would lose the information that the length is finite. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.CharacterBoxes.ExistsPageN")[Finiteness and every finite subtotal.]

These quantifiers have a useful elementary consequence. Suppose $B$ is finite and the cutoff fixed. For every prime contributing positively, the subtotal over that singleton is at least $log 2$. If there were $m$ such primes, their finite subtotal would be at least $m log 2$. Hence at most $floor(B/(log 2))$ primes contribute positively, provided $B>=0$. In addition, a positive contribution at $ell$ forces $log ell<=B$. These facts need no explicit computation of an infinite sum. Taking the supremum over the finite subtotals is especially convenient when the finite set of supporting primes changes with the arithmetic data or admits no sharp description.

A small example checks both the length and the logarithmic weight. Suppose the two nonzero twist lengths at the prime three are one and two and the only other nonzero length is two at five. For a finite set containing both primes the subtotal is $3log 3+2log 5=log(675)$. A singleton containing five contributes $log(25)$, not the integer two. Resetting the cutoff to discard the twist of length two at three reduces the subtotal by $2log 3$ if all other listed twists remain. Increasing the right-hand budget at the same time can never reverse the truth of a previous upper bound. This is the monotonicity used when passing from the raw cutoff to the physical cutoff.

It is also important that the field is fixed for each application of this assertion. The next statement quantifies over every field satisfying a specified cyclotomic fixed-field condition at that layer. The window theorem puts this assertion inside a universal quantifier over all points of the raw interval. The field may change between points. The fixed-field condition allows the bound to be applied to the particular field used to construct the theta data. Thus one can simultaneously read the universal quantifier as providing flexibility and its fixed-field hypothesis as a restriction on that flexibility.

#heading(level:2,numbering:none)[Tracing a conclusion back through its inputs]

The following table translates the arguments of one theorem on the definite-side theta construction. Its conclusion is the right-half-plane property for all characters. Each row lists the role of a hypothesis and a mathematical place to examine it. A statement of this form is an implication. To reach the public conclusion the development must also supply all seven inputs. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.ThetaCorrespondence.ThetaPairLifts.ThirdPlaceSeries.SiegelEisensteinSecondTermHypotheses")[The seven-input theorem.]

