A useful exercise is to follow the last input through one more implication. Given a primitive character $psi$, fix $epsilon$ and a function choosing a two-power layer at every raw $x>=1$. The raw window property returns $J$ and an outer threshold before it learns the outer scale $X$ or the pair $q,d$. For every sufficiently large $X$ and every pair satisfying the requirements at $X^(1/J)$, it returns an admissible interval inside that scale. This universal quantifier over good pairs is what permits another part of the argument to pick the pair by Fourier estimates. If the window theorem selected its own independent pair, one would need an additional simultaneous-selection argument. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.CharacterBoxes.ExistsPageN")[The raw window statement.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.CharacterBoxes.WinPairs")[Using a selected pair.]

At the final assembly, one Galois compatibility theorem enters more than once. It is used by the automorphic comparison and to furnish the cohomological window. The constructor also supplies the local-parameter result. Reusing one theorem in two deductions does not create a logical circle. In this case its hypotheses concern representations and local data. Its use later has as conclusion a statement about zeros of Dirichlet functions. Looking up the precise arguments prevents a phrase such as “using the transfer theorem” from hiding which direction of implication is intended. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.CharacterBoxes.MainConclusion")[Final composition.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.Foundations.GaloisRepresentations.IntegralStructures.RowSrc")[Supply of the Galois theorem.]

#heading(level:2,numbering:none)[What an independent check records]

There are two independently meaningful comparisons. One compares the formal conclusion with the mathematical proposition of interest, as in the first section of this appendix. Another checks that the terms in a specified release have the claimed types using the stated foundational axioms. A correct proof of an incorrectly specified proposition would answer a different mathematical question. Conversely a correct definition by itself supplies no proof term.

An independent statement check made for this book imported four mathlib modules in the pinned v4.33.1 environment. It formalized the textbook condition using holomorphy off one, the Dirichlet series on $Re s>1$, and the negative even integers. The theorem `RHStatementAudit.mathlib_iff_textbook` proves both directions of equivalence with mathlib's `RiemannHypothesis`. This tests the mathematical target without importing the submitted proof. The Clay Mathematics Institute gives the customary formulation together with Bombieri's official problem description. #link("https://www.claymath.org/millennium/riemann-hypothesis/")[Clay's problem description.]

Andrew Sutherland's record, updated on 21 November 2026 at 12:55 UTC, reports a successful build and Lean kernel replay of all 175,187 modules. Two independently implemented checkers, nanoda and con-leche, accepted the full export. A separate con-leche comparison matched all five conclusions to the challenge. Their dependencies were propositional extensionality (`propext`), classical choice (`Classical.choice`) and soundness of quotients (`Quot.sound`). The mathlib files also matched the official cache byte for byte. At the recorded time the end-to-end `lake comparator` run and Lean's own replay of the single export were still in progress. Sutherland reports these full machine runs. #link("https://math.mit.edu/~drew/grh/")[Sutherland's verification record.] #link("https://github.com/leanprover-community/riemann-hypothesis/tree/40e56bab026552e089d7afcfeff08d2defb9c005")[Source snapshot used in this book.]

These checks cover the published proof and its mathematical statement. Universal soundness of a checker on every possible input is a separate theorem.

